{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HsyGzWeLaD2U6i3Bev5JNn","type":"tool_result","content":"title:\tBuild leave-web-app React frontend\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\txlight05\nlabels:\tasdlc, implementation, pending\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\tmekanamwedakarannaone2 (In Progress)\nmilestone:\t\nnumber:\t2\n--\n## Component Constraints\n- **Name:** leave-web-app\n- **Type:** web-app\n- **Language/Stack:** TypeScript / React\n- **App Path (within repo):** `/leave-web-app`\n\n## Responsibilities\n- Provide user interface for employees to submit leave requests with date selection and leave type dropdown\n- Display employee's submitted leave requests with current status (pending/approved/rejected)\n- Show employee's remaining leave balance by leave type in dashboard\n- Provide manager interface to view pending leave requests from direct reports\n- Enable managers to approve or reject leave requests with comments\n- Implement role-based view switching between employee and manager interfaces\n- Client-side validation of leave request forms including date range and required fields\n- Integrate with leave-service REST API for all data operations\n- Display loading states and error messages with user-friendly notifications\n- Implement health check endpoint for monitoring\n- Serve application via nginx with API proxying to backend service\n\n## Key Considerations\n- Use React Context for managing user role and authentication state\n- Implement proper error boundaries and loading states for better UX\n- Date pickers must prevent selection of past dates to avoid invalid submissions\n- Client-side validation should match backend validation rules\n- API calls should include proper error handling with retry logic where appropriate\n- Nginx configuration must correctly proxy /api routes to leave-service while serving React app for all other routes\n- Environment variable REACT_APP_API_URL must be configurable for different deployment environments\n- Component should be responsive and work on mobile and desktop browsers\n- Use TypeScript for type safety across all components and API integrations\n- Docker image should be optimized for production with multi-stage builds\n\n## Component Dependencies\nThis component depends on the following components. You MUST:\n1. Declare each as a dependency in `workload.yaml` — OpenChoreo injects the resolved URL as an environment variable at runtime.\n2. Use the injected environment variable in your application code — **never hardcode service URLs**.\n\n- **leave-service** → use env var `LEAVE_SERVICE_URL`\n\nAdd this to your `workload.yaml`:\n\n```yaml\ndependencies:\n  endpoints:\n    - component: leave-service\n      name: http\n      visibility: project\n      envBindings:\n        address: LEAVE_SERVICE_URL\n```\n\n## Project Structure Requirements\nCreate a production-ready project structure in the current directory:\n- package.json with dependencies and scripts\n- tsconfig.json\n- src/ with App component and entry point\n- vite.config.ts\n- Dockerfile (build + nginx for serving)\n\nCreate a `workload.yaml` at the root of your component directory to declare endpoints and dependencies. Refer to the OpenChoreo workload configuration guidance in your skill for the correct format, endpoint types, dependency wiring, and the SPA nginx proxy pattern for frontend components.\n\nThe platform will handle git commit, push, build, and deploy automatically.\n## OpenAPI Spec\n\n```yaml\nopenapi: 3.0.3\ninfo:\n  title: Leave Management Web App\n  version: 1.0.0\n  description: Frontend application for leave management\npaths:\n  /health:\n    get:\n      summary: Health check\n      responses:\n        '200':\n          description: OK\n          content:\n            application/json:\n              schema:\n                type: object\n                properties:\n                  status:\n                    type: string\n                    example: healthy\n```\n\n## Agent Instructions\nBuild a React 18+ single-page application using TypeScript and Vite as the build tool running on port 3000. Implement two main views: (1) Employee View with a form to submit leave requests (date pickers for start/end dates, dropdown for leave type selection), a table showing submitted requests with status badges, and a dashboard card displaying remaining balance per leave type. (2) Manager View with a table of pending requests from direct reports, approve/reject buttons per request, and filters by employee or date range. Use React Router for navigation between employee and manager views. Integrate with leave-service backend at environment variable REACT_APP_API_URL using fetch or axios for API calls. Implement error handling with toast notifications. Style with Tailwind CSS or Material-UI. Store user role (employee/manager) and ID in React Context after login simulation (hardcoded for MVP). Add loading spinners during API calls. Date picker should prevent past dates. Validate form inputs client-side before submission. Display leave types fetched from backend. Show success/error messages after actions. Implement a /health endpoint that returns {status: 'healthy'}. Create Dockerfile using node:18-alpine base image, run npm install and npm run build, and serve static files with nginx on port 3000. Include nginx.conf to proxy /api calls to leave-service and serve React app on all other routes. Ensure all OpenAPI spec endpoints are consumed correctly including GET /health, GET /api/leave-types, GET /api/employees/{employeeId}/balance, POST /api/leave-requests, GET /api/leave-requests with query filters, and POST /api/leave-requests/{requestId}/approve and /reject endpoints.\n\n## General Constraints\n- Write ALL files in the current working directory (do NOT create files outside it).\n- The component must have a Dockerfile for containerized builds.\n- Implement the full API contract from the task — every endpoint must be functional.\n- Use the dependencies listed to understand what other services this component talks to.\n- The app MUST start without any required environment variables — use sensible hardcoded defaults for all config (JWT secrets, DB paths, API URLs, etc.). Environment variables may override defaults but must never be required.\n- Do NOT stub or mock — write real, working implementations.\n- Do NOT run, start, or execute the application server locally. Only write source files. The platform builds and deploys your code automatically — local execution is unnecessary and will cause port conflicts.\n- If you must run a quick compile check (e.g. `go build`, `tsc --noEmit`), do so without starting a server. Never use `go run`, `npm start`, `node server.js`, or any command that starts a long-running process.\n\n## Working Directory\nAll file operations must happen inside this directory:\n\n```\n/Users/senith/.asdlc/repos/default/mekanamwedakarannaone2/leave-web-app\n```\n\nBefore you do anything else, navigate there:\n\n```bash\ncd /Users/senith/.asdlc/repos/default/mekanamwedakarannaone2/leave-web-app\n```\n\nDo NOT create files outside this directory. Do NOT `cd` elsewhere during your work.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"74582627-f9b4-4bf2-823b-05bb42d3c266","uuid":"062c4cf8-8a61-44c5-afe5-69bcfb2eac2e","timestamp":"2026-05-02T20:01:13.098Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"title:\tBuild leave-web-app React frontend\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\txlight05\nlabels:\tasdlc, implementation, pending\ncomments:\t0\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\tmekanamwedakarannaone2 (In Progress)\nmilestone:\t\nnumber:\t2\n--\n## Component Constraints\n- **Name:** leave-web-app\n- **Type:** web-app\n- **Language/Stack:** TypeScript / React\n- **App Path (within repo):** `/leave-web-app`\n\n## Responsibilities\n- Provide user interface for employees to submit leave requests with date selection and leave type dropdown\n- Display employee's submitted leave requests with current status (pending/approved/rejecte